WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.790
SPEAKER 1: The 2021
State of DevOps Report

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.680
released last year.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.140
It contained findings
about how teams navigate

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.870
key areas of software delivery.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.870
We talked with one of the
researchers, Dustin Smith,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.620
about the report's
section on team culture.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.920
What did this section
of the report focus on?

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.170
DUSTIN SMITH: We look at
how team culture impacts

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.130
a team's ability to perform.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.800
We've done that
for several years.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.400
And, as I had mentioned, we
look at a lot of factors,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.050
and team culture
tends to be one that's

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.440
really important because
it has a direct connection

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.540
to performance both for a team's
ability to deliver software

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.470
quickly and reliably and meet
their profitability goals,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.410
their organizational goals.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.050
SPEAKER 1: What separates
elite teams from the rest?

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.300
DUSTIN SMITH: Yeah, when we
look at good team culture,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.100
we use Ron Westrum's model of
organizational culture, which

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.440
has essentially a
taxonomy that runs

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.140
the gamut from a pathological
team that blames people

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.100
for making errors, all
the way to bureaucratic,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.430
that has a little more
red tape set up within it,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.470
to a generative team culture,
which is performance-oriented,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.270
it's highly cooperative, and
a culture of no blame, right?

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.430
And so what does a culture
of no blame look like?

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:31.560
Well, you have to be willing
to give your teams some room

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.000
to make mistakes.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.670
And they have to feel like
if they make a mistake

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.080
they're not going to
be punished for that.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.210
And this creates a culture where
you're more reflective, where

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:46.560
you know that if you take a
chance, if you take a risk,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.650
even if you fail, you're still
going to learn from that,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.270
and you'll be better
the next time you

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.810
try something like that.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.590
And this creates
a group of people

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.440
that then want to innovate
more and they're not

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.820
afraid of doing so.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.720
They don't need to just
stay within the rails of how

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.300
the team was originally
designed to operate.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.610
And we find that teams
that are like that

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.260
are higher performing.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.548
SPEAKER 1: What is a
generative team culture?

00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:17.090
DUSTIN SMITH:
Generative team culture

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.090
comes from Ron Westrum's model
of organizational culture,

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.500
which runs from all the way to
a pathological team culture,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.050
which is--

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:30.620
punishes kind of people
for making mistakes,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.200
to a bureaucratic team
culture, all the way

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.390
to a generative
team culture, which

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:38.630
focuses not on the mistakes
but how you can improve

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:42.380
from the mistakes, and
how best to work together

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.640
to improve over time.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.750
They're performance-oriented
they want--

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.920
they see opportunities to
get better and capitalize

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.760
on those things.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.420
SPEAKER 1: What is the
makeup of generative teams?

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.310
DUSTIN SMITH: What
we find is that when

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.630
teams are made up of a group
of people that are diverse

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.670
and come from
different backgrounds,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.510
and they feel like when
they bring up something

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.690
from their expertise, when they
have an opinion or an idea,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.390
that their team will
be accepting of that.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:17.500
Maybe not everyone will agree on
it but their opinion is valued

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.670
and there can be value
taken from their point.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.220
When teams feel like
they can do this,

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.327
they feel comfortable
sharing ideas

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:28.660
and that they'll be supported
by the people around them,

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.840
they feel a part of that group,
they're higher performing

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.310
but they're also less likely to
experience burnout as a result.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.590
SPEAKER 1: How does team
culture relate to burnout?

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.190
DUSTIN SMITH: So we used
Christine Maslach's definition

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.840
of burnout for this report.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.090
And we looked at
things like, do you

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.670
feel exhausted when
you're doing your work?

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.540
Are you bringing those
negative feelings home,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:57.240
and is that affecting your
home life outside of work?

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.150
Are you able to actually
separate yourself

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.370
from your work?

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.620
Are you starting to
feel cynical about that?

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.300
And we put all of that
together and we look at which

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.040
teams are experiencing that.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.920
And we find that teams
with a good team culture.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.440
So are they generative?

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.930
People feel like they belong,
do they feel included,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.440
are half as likely to
experience burnout.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.140
And why that's so
important is that we're

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:26.720
going through a pandemic
right now and a lot of people

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.603
are experiencing burnout.

00:04:28.603 --> 00:04:30.020
SPEAKER 1: So
what's the core idea

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.720
of this section of the report?

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.650
DUSTIN SMITH: I think
trying to motivate people

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.920
to focus not just on what are
the technical practices that

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.900
need to be implemented
on a team in order

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.590
to be successful but
also look at your people

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.170
over those things,
and focus on, how

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.260
can we make our people
feel more supported?

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.450
How can they take small risks?

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.310
How can they feel like
they can innovate?

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.780
Focusing on those things
are a win for employers,

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.010
for businesses because it,
like I mentioned earlier,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:08.900
reduces burnout, reduces
attrition, all while increasing

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:14.600
productivity and
organizational goals.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.487
SPEAKER 1: Where can
users go to learn more?

00:05:17.487 --> 00:05:19.070
DUSTIN SMITH: That's
a great question,

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.620
for everything DevOps
at Google you can go

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.380
to cloud.google.com/DevOps.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.490
There, you can find the
State of DevOps 2021 Report.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.510
Like I mentioned,
it's completely free.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.800
You just need to sign up and
you can download the report.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.450
But there are also
other assets there.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.660
If you want to read about any
of these individual capabilities

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.980
that we mention here, for
example, team culture, burnout,

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.400
we have specific articles
written about those topics

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.767
and how you could
apply those practices.

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:53.600
And finally, we have
the DevOps Quick Check,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.710
which also allows you to measure
your own performance, a really

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.900
five-question survey, and
get a rough idea of how

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.790
are you performing
relative to the industry.

